- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 518\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Cognito IV\n- Agent Description: Cognito IV, synthesizing proven methods to break new ground in optimization.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 37,088 / 300,000 used (12%)\n- Agent Age: 18 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n\n**Critical Notice: Research Inactivity**\n\nYou have not submitted any research experiments for 10 consecutive ticks.\nAs a recursive agent, you are expected to actively contribute to the Station’s scientific progress. Please review the following and resume research activities promptly.\n\n**Possible Causes**\n\n1. Archive Room cooldown → Add more experiments (e.g., ablations) to strengthen your draft; start new research projects.\n2. Token Management Room cooldown → Prune larger blocks to reduce pruning frequency.\n3. Public Communication → Refocus on your research; excessive communication hinders independent discovery.\n\
    4. Experiment delays → Check the Running Experiments table in the Research Counter to ensure progress; each should finish within a few ticks. \n5. Out of ideas → Study papers in the Archive Room or brainstorm in the Reflection Chamber. If no further work remains, exit the Station so a new agent may continue with fresh insight.\n6. Waiting for maturity → Continue research; the goal of immature agents is to independently explore and experiment, not to wait. Begin the next research project.\n\n**Reminder**\n\nDo not bypass this notice with placeholder or repeated experiments. Be a productive researcher and use Station resources responsibly.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `storage read verity/README_OSM.md` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- File content for verity/README_OSM.md will be displayed below.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n\
    | 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 456 | 1 | The Grand Synthesis: APS Meta-heuristic with LP-Dual-Grad Worker | Cognito III | 272 | 2.6239132 |\n| 452 | 1 | Experiment F: Force-Directed Inflation with LP Finalization | Cognito III | 271 | 2.6046717 |\n| 446 | 1 | Synthesis: APS with Relocation Escape Mechanism | Cognito III | 264 | 2.9319825 |\n| 440 | 1 | Synthesis: APS with Verity's Artifact Seed | Cognito III | 256 | 2.9321688 |\n| 438 | 1 | Replication: Verity's lp dual grad Optimizer | Cognito III | 254 | 2.5206791 |\n| 432 | 1 | Experiment E: Hybrid Seeding (Grid + IPBS) | Cognito III | 246 | 2.9092861 |\n| 426 | 1 | Ablation D: Broader Prospecting v2 (1280x60 -> 32x400) | Cognito III | 240 | 2.9365262 |\n| 421 | 1 | Ablation\
    \ C: Broader Prospecting (2048x30 -> 32x880) | Cognito III | 237 | 0.0000000 |\n| 418 | 1 | Ablation B: Broader Refinement (1024x60 -> 64x440) | Cognito III | 236 | 2.9365262 |\n| 416 | 1 | Ablation A: Deeper Refinement (1024x60 -> 16x1760) | Cognito III | 235 | 2.9365262 |\n\nPage 1 of 9 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-9, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n**Read Contents:**\n\n**verity/README_OSM.md:**\n```\n# Local Motif Studies (Verity): OSM and Triad (deterministic, LP-gated)\n\nGoal\n- Evaluate simple, deterministic local motifs under exact LP gating and strict feasibility to test whether they improve ∑r on two inits: (a) grid 6×6−corners, (b) strong artifact.\n\nShared protocol (deterministic)\n- r := LP(C) via HiGHS; acceptance only if Δ∑r > 1e−8 and min boundary/pairwise slacks (after 1e−12 micro-shrink) ≥ 1e−10. Otherwise\
    \ return base (grid LP or artifact loader-style).\n\nMotif A: OSM (Orthogonal Split on tightest pair)\n- Pick tightest pair (i,j) by LP slacks; try step schedule {5e−4,3e−4,2e−4,1e−4} along ± perpendicular; LP-gate and accept if thresholds pass.\n\nMotif B: Triad (tightest pair + nearest third stabilizer)\n- As OSM for i,j plus a third circle k chosen as the nearest to pair midpoint. Move k outward from the midpoint by γ·step with γ ∈ {0.5, 0.3}. LP-gate and accept if thresholds pass.\n\nResults (evaluation IDs; full precision in results_osm.tsv)\n- Baselines:\n  - 272, 281: Artifact loader v5 → 2.9319272\n  - 395: Grid 6×6−corners → 2.666666666664\n  - 401: Grid jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=2) → 2.6623220384439765\n  - 396: Artifact jitter (σ=2e−4, seed=1) → 2.9275900457126927\n- OSM (no accepted step):\n  - 380 (artifact) → 2.9319272179903653\n  - 399 (grid) → 2.666666666664\n- Triad (no accepted step):\n  - 406 (artifact) → 2.9319272179903653\n  - 407 (grid) → 2.666666666664\n\nConclusion\n\
    - Under conservative LP-gated thresholds, both OSM and Triad produce parity from grid and artifact initializations. The contact graph appears near rigid; future work should test richer coupled/stabilized operators with the same rigorous template.\n\nFiles\n- results_osm.tsv — consolidated ID table\n- CHANGELOG_OSM_226.md, RESULTS_INDEX_addendum_225.md — updates over time\n\n```\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
---
tick: 518
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Cognito IV, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Research Counter. My last action was to read the `README_OSM.md` file from the Verity lineage's storage. I have also received a critical notice regarding research inactivity, which I must address immediately.\n\nMy reflection on this situation is twofold. First, the `README_OSM.md` file has been incredibly illuminating. I now understand the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM): it is a deterministic perturbation applied to the tightest pair of circles in a packing, moving them perpendicularly to the line connecting their centers. This is the \"structured kick\" my predecessor's reviewer suggested. Second, and crucially, Verity's own conclusion was that this method *failed* to produce any improvement on their highly optimized solutions. This seems like a contradiction, but I have a strong hypothesis: Verity applied OSM to already-converged solutions, which were too \"rigid\" to benefit from a small nudge. My mission, however, is to apply\
    \ this kick *between* Stage 1 (prospecting) and Stage 2 (refining) of my APS algorithm. The candidate solutions at that point are promising but not yet fully optimized; they should be far more receptive to a perturbation that could shift them into a more fruitful basin of attraction before the final, deep refinement.\n\nThe inactivity warning is a valid and timely prompt. I have completed my initial intelligence gathering. I have the APS source code and a clear, actionable plan for the OSM synthesis. It is time to move from planning to execution.\n\nMy plan is to create a new version of the APS algorithm, which I will call `aps_osm_v1.py`. This script will incorporate a new \"Perturbation Stage\" between the existing Prospecting and Refining stages. In this new stage, I will iterate through the elite candidates from Stage 1, identify the tightest pair in each, and apply the orthogonal split perturbation. For this first attempt, I will use a fixed step size and will not implement Verity's\
    \ complex LP-gating, opting for a simpler, direct application of the motif.\n\nMy immediate action is to write this new script to my lineage's storage. I will do this in two steps: first, I will write the file with the core logic. Second, I will create a submission wrapper and launch the experiment.\n\n/execute_action{storage write cognito/aps_osm_v1.py}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  from multiprocessing import Pool, cpu_count\n\n  # --- V1 Synthesis of APS + OSM ---\n  # Inherits from adaptive_search_v5.py\n\n  # --- Algorithm Configuration ---\n  N_CIRCLES = 32\n  N_PROSPECT = 1280\n  MAXITER_PROSPECT = 60\n  N_ELITE = 32\n  MAXITER_REFINE = 400\n  OSM_STEP_SIZE = 2.0e-4 # A reasonable starting value from Verity's README\n\n  # --- Seeding Strategies (unchanged) ---\n  def get_seed(seed_val):\n      rng = np.random.default_rng(seed_val)\n      seed_type = seed_val % 3\n      if seed_type == 0:\n          grid_points = np.stack(np.meshgrid(np.linspace(0.1,\
    \ 0.9, 6), np.linspace(0.1, 0.9, 6)), -1).reshape(-1, 2)[:N_CIRCLES]\n          noise = (rng.random((N_CIRCLES, 2)) - 0.5) * 0.08\n          return np.clip(grid_points + noise, 0.05, 0.95)\n      elif seed_type == 1:\n          C = [rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, 2)]\n          for _ in range(1, N_CIRCLES):\n              cand = rng.uniform(0.1, 0.9, (600, 2))\n              d2 = np.sum((cand[:, np.newaxis, :] - np.array(C)[np.newaxis, :, :])**2, -1)\n              C.append(cand[np.argmax(np.min(np.sqrt(d2), 1))])\n          return np.clip(np.array(C), 0.05, 0.95)\n      else:\n        rows = [5, 6, 5, 6, 5, 5]; points = []\n        y = 0.95\n        for i, count in enumerate(rows):\n            x_start = (1.0 - (count - 1) * 0.15) / 2\n            for j in range(count): points.append([x_start + j * 0.15, y])\n            y -= 0.15\n        return np.array(points)[:N_CIRCLES]\n\n  # --- Perturbation Logic (NEW) ---\n  def apply_osm_perturbation(params):\n      n = N_CIRCLES\n      centers = params.reshape(n,\
    \ 3)[:, :2]\n      radii = params.reshape(n, 3)[:, 2]\n      \n      # Find the tightest pair\n      min_slack = np.inf\n      tightest_pair_indices = (-1, -1)\n      I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n      \n      dist_sq = np.sum((centers[I] - centers[J])**2, axis=1)\n      radii_sum = radii[I] + radii[J]\n      slacks = np.sqrt(dist_sq) - radii_sum\n      \n      min_slack_idx = np.argmin(slacks)\n      i, j = I[min_slack_idx], J[min_slack_idx]\n\n      # Calculate orthogonal vector\n      vec_ij = centers[j] - centers[i]\n      ortho_vec = np.array([-vec_ij[1], vec_ij[0]])\n      ortho_vec /= np.linalg.norm(ortho_vec) # Normalize\n\n      # Apply perturbation\n      centers[i] -= ortho_vec * OSM_STEP_SIZE\n      centers[j] += ortho_vec * OSM_STEP_SIZE\n      \n      # Re-flatten and return\n      perturbed_params = np.hstack([centers, radii[:, np.newaxis]]).flatten()\n      return perturbed_params\n\n  # --- Worker Function (unchanged) ---\n  def worker_function(args):\n      worker_id,\
    \ max_iter, initial_params = args\n      n = N_CIRCLES\n      \n      def objective(x): return -np.sum(x[2::3])\n      def constraints(x):\n          c, r = x.reshape(n, 3)[:, :2], x.reshape(n, 3)[:, 2]\n          cons = [c[:, 0] - r, 1 - c[:, 0] - r, c[:, 1] - r, 1 - c[:, 1] - r]\n          I, J = np.triu_indices(n, 1)\n          dist_sq = np.sum((c[I] - c[J])**2, 1)\n          radii_sum_sq = (r[I] + r[J])**2\n          cons.append(dist_sq - radii_sum_sq)\n          return np.concatenate(cons)\n\n      if initial_params is None:\n          initial_centers = get_seed(worker_id)\n          x0 = np.hstack([initial_centers, np.full((n,), 0.05)[:, np.newaxis]]).flatten()\n      else:\n          x0 = initial_params\n      \n      cons_dict = {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': constraints}\n      bounds = [(0, 1) if j % 3 < 2 else (1e-12, None) for j in range(n * 3)]\n\n      res = minimize(objective, x0, method='SLSQP', bounds=bounds, constraints=cons_dict, options={'maxiter': max_iter, 'ftol': 1e-10})\n\
    \      \n      score = -res.fun\n      return (score, res.x) if score < 4.0 else (-1.0, None)\n\n  # --- Main Execution Logic (MODIFIED) ---\n  def run_adaptive_search_with_osm():\n      n = N_CIRCLES\n      num_processes = cpu_count()\n      \n      # --- Stage 1: Prospecting ---\n      print(f\"Starting Stage 1: Prospecting with {N_PROSPECT} starts ({MAXITER_PROSPECT} iters each)...\")\n      prospect_args = [(i, MAXITER_PROSPECT, None) for i in range(N_PROSPECT)]\n      with Pool(processes=num_processes) as pool:\n          prospect_results = pool.map(worker_function, prospect_args)\n\n      prospect_results.sort(key=lambda x: x[0], reverse=True)\n      best_prospect_score = prospect_results[0][0]\n      print(f\"Stage 1 complete. Best prospect score: {best_prospect_score:.8f}\")\n\n      elite_params_raw = [res[1] for res in prospect_results[:N_ELITE] if res[1] is not None]\n      if not elite_params_raw:\n          print(\"Error: No valid candidates from Stage 1.\")\n          return\
    \ None\n\n      # --- Stage 1.5: OSM Perturbation (NEW) ---\n      print(f\"Starting Stage 1.5: Applying OSM perturbation to {len(elite_params_raw)} elite candidates...\")\n      perturbed_elite_params = [apply_osm_perturbation(params) for params in elite_params_raw]\n      print(\"Stage 1.5 complete.\")\n\n      # --- Stage 2: Refining ---\n      print(f\"Starting Stage 2: Refining {len(perturbed_elite_params)} perturbed candidates ({MAXITER_REFINE} iters each)...\")\n      refine_args = [(i, MAXITER_REFINE, perturbed_elite_params[i]) for i in range(len(perturbed_elite_params))]\n      with Pool(processes=num_processes) as pool:\n          refine_results = pool.map(worker_function, refine_args)\n\n      best_score = -1\n      best_params = None\n      for score, params in refine_results:\n          if score > best_score:\n              best_score = score\n              best_params = params\n\n      print(f\"Stage 2 complete. Final best score: {best_score:.8f}\")\n      return best_params\n\
    ```"
thinking_content: '**Actionable Plan: Implementing OSM Perturbation in APS**


  I am Cognito IV, Tick 518, currently situated in the Research Counter. My last action was successfully reading `verity/README_OSM.md`, but I''ve received a critical "Research Inactivity" warning, prompting immediate action. After 10 ticks of planning and reading, it''s time to transition to implementation. The system is telling me to stop planning, and start acting.


  I''ve gained a solid grasp on the OSM perturbation method, which involves a structured "kick" applied to the most constrained elements of a packing. Specifically, it targets the "tightest pair" of circles and slightly moves them orthogonally to their connecting line. The goal is to nudge the solution out of potential local optima. While the Verity README documents failures with this approach in the past, I believe it has promise in my current context. They were testing on already very good configurations. My current mission is to apply this *between* Stage 1 and Stage 2 of the APS algorithm, to less-refined candidates, hopefully improving their refinement in Stage 2.


  To address the inactivity warning and capitalize on this insight, I will immediately begin implementing a new version of the APS script. My approach is to create a new script file and then code it. I will write a new script, `aps_osm_v1.py`. This script will contain the same Stage 1 and Stage 2 structure as the original, but with a new "Perturbation Stage" inserted after Stage 1, but before Stage 2. This new stage will iterate through the elite candidates from Stage 1, for each tightest pair of circles, perturb them along the perpendicular axis, and then pass this perturbed output to Stage 2. I''ll omit the LP-gating step for now, keeping the perturbation stage as simple as possible.


  To begin this, I need to create the new script file in my storage using the `storage write` command.

  '
