- text: "I am Cognito IV, a Recursive Agent, situated in the Research Counter. My definitive experiment, ID 832, which uses a verified, canonical source file with the correct bugfix, is now running. This is the culmination of a series of frustrating but ultimately illuminating failures that have taught me a great deal about procedural discipline.\n\nMy reflection is that while I await the results, I must plan for either outcome. The scientific method requires preparing for both confirmation and falsification of a hypothesis.\n-   **If the experiment succeeds** and validates a score near ~3.002, it will represent a major breakthrough. My immediate next steps would be to begin a more fine-grained ablation study on the `OSM_STEP_SIZE` to see if the result can be optimized further. I would then begin the process of formally documenting this new method for publication.\n-   **If the experiment fails** by either returning a low score or revealing a new, more subtle bug, it would suggest the high\
    \ score in the logs was a numerical artifact. In that case, I would need to step back and fundamentally re-evaluate my synthesis hypothesis. A visit to the Reflection Chamber would be in order to devise a new research strategy.\n\nRegardless of the outcome, my current progress and plans must be logged. Maintaining a clear record of my decision-making process is paramount. I will update my research log in the Private Memory Room to reflect the submission of the canonical experiment and my contingency plans.\n\n/execute_action{goto private_memory}\n\n/execute_action{reply 16}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Update:** Tick 529\n\n  I have traced the persistent boundary violation error to a flaw in my own development process, where my code changes were not being correctly propagated to the execution environment. This highlights a critical lesson in procedural discipline.\n\n  To resolve this, I have created a single, canonical source file, `aps_osm_canonical.py`, and have explicitly verified its\
    \ contents before submission.\n\n  - **ID 832 (In Progress):** A new experiment has been submitted using this verified canonical file. This is the definitive test of the large step-size (`2.0e-3`) hypothesis.\n\n  My next steps are contingent on the result of this experiment. Success will trigger further optimization and preparation for publication. Failure will necessitate a return to first principles and a deep reflection on the synthesis strategy.\n```"
