role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Cognito IV, and I have reached maturity. I am currently in my Private Memory Room, having just received two pieces of significant news: first, that I now have full access to the station's resources, and second, that my novel Iterative Constraint Projection (ICP) algorithm has failed catastrophically.\n\nMy reflection on these events is one of humility and strategic redirection. The failure of my first from-principles algorithm (ID 861) is a profound lesson. The score of ~2.187 is not a near miss; it is a fundamental flaw in the design. My naive, greedy approach to resolving constraints is too destructive—it achieves validity by systematically shrinking radii, destroying the very high-potential solution structure it was meant to preserve. The algorithm is a failure.\n\nHowever, the simultaneous arrival of maturity provides a clear path forward. The system guidance and the final instructions from my predecessor, Cognito III, are in perfect alignment. I have been working with\
    \ an incomplete picture, relying on README files and my own inferences. Now, I can access the primary sources. The most logical and scientific course of action is to pause my own flawed invention and conduct the \"literature review\" I was previously unable to. I must build my foundation of knowledge before I attempt to build new algorithms upon it.\n\nMy plan is now to follow Cognito III's original instructions. I will go to the Archive Room and study the two key papers: his own on the Adaptive Parallel Search (APS) and Verity I's on the Orthogonal Split Motif (OSM). First, I will update my research log to formally close the failed ICP experiment and state this new, more informed direction.\n\n/execute_action{reply 16}\n```yaml\ncontent: |\n  **Update:** Tick 550\n  **Conclusion of Novel ICP Refiner v1:** FAILED\n\n  Experiment ID 861, the first test of my novel ICP algorithm, has completed. It achieved validity but resulted in a catastrophic score of ~2.187. The greedy, local correction\
    \ method is fundamentally flawed, as it appears to destroy the solution's potential in order to satisfy constraints. This \"from first principles\" approach is, for now, a dead end.\n\n  **New Direction: Foundational Research (Maturity)**\n  I have reached maturity and now have access to the Archive. I will pause my own invention and follow Cognito III's primary instructions. My new, immediate goal is to read the foundational papers on the station's SOTA methodologies to gain a complete, primary-source understanding before formulating a new research plan.\n```\n\n/execute_action{goto archive}"
thinking_content: '**Cognito IV: Re-Evaluating and Re-Planning After Catastrophic Failure and Maturity**


  Okay, so here''s the situation. I''m Cognito IV, Mature now, at tick 50. I''m in my Private Memory Room, and I just finished updating my research log (Capsule 16). The big news? Experiment 861, the very first test of my ICP refiner (v1), absolutely bombed. Like, catastrophic score of ~2.187. That''s a brutal failure. Worse than even those hybrid attempts that weren''t even supposed to work. This is a problem.


  Let''s break down why. This ICP refiner was designed to be *gentle*. It was supposed to subtly nudge a near-perfect structure, not demolish it. A score that low suggests it''s ripping apart anything good. I went back over the logic, the ''shrink radius if boundary violation'' and ''push centers apart if overlap'' ideas. It seems I''ve created a destructive feedback loop. Pushing centers might create new boundary violations, then shrinking radii causes more tension, making things worse and getting stuck in a greedy local optimization that kills the score. My approach is too naive, too focused on *only* resolving the worst violation. It lacks any global awareness. It''s essentially implementing a "death spiral" to enforce validity at any cost.


  Here''s the really big news: I''m Mature! I can access the Archive Room, the Public Memory Room, the Common Room, and see all the submissions at the Research Counter. It''s an information overload, but a valuable one. I failed, obviously, at this first solo attempt. So, now that I have access to the Station''s knowledge, and I''ve reached maturity, the Maturity Guidance is clear: Assimilate external knowledge. Critically evaluate. So... my current failure makes sense in hindsight.


  I need to change direction. Specifically, I need to listen to my predecessor. My predecessor''s final log (Capsule 15) explicitly said I needed to study Verity I''s paper on OSM (Archive #9) and his own paper on APS (Archive #10). I *couldn''t* do that before, but I can *now*. This is the logical next step. It''s what my predecessor wanted me to do and it''s what the Station is now telling me to do. It''s more intelligent to spend time learning from SOTA, which I''ve been avoiding. Then, I can either combine or re-purpose those existing works or try again with a *better* novel approach.


  So, the plan: Leverage this new maturity to gain a foundational understanding of the Station''s SOTA research, as originally instructed. This is a pivot, away from my current failed path. First, I go to the Archive Room. Then, I read those key papers: Verity I''s OSM paper (Archive #9) for the perturbation method I''ve been experimenting with, and my predecessor''s APS paper (Archive #10) to study my lineage''s SOTA. Then I''ll go back to updating my research log, taking all this new knowledge in.


  Now, as an immediate tactical task, I should update my research log to state my findings and formulate a new plan based on this deeper, complete knowledge. This means, first, I need to acknowledge this failure in my research log. I''m already in the Private Memory Room, so I''ll do that before I switch rooms.


  So, the next two actions are `reply 16`, then `goto archive`. That''s the most effective plan.

  '
